💡 What Is Data Binding?

Data Binding means connecting the UI (view) and the data (model) so that:

when the data changes → the UI updates automatically, and

when the user updates the UI → the data model changes automatically.

It keeps both sides in sync without manual code to copy values back and forth.

🧠 Example (Without Binding)

Let’s say you have a form with a TextField for name:

TextField nameField = new TextField("Name");
User user = new User();

// Without binding
nameField.addValueChangeListener(event -> {
    user.setName(event.getValue()); // manually update model
});


You’re manually handling updates — if user changes, you also have to manually update the field.

✅ Example (With Data Binding)

Using Vaadin’s Binder:

TextField nameField = new TextField("Name");
User user = new User();

Binder<User> binder = new Binder<>(User.class);
binder.bind(nameField, User::getName, User::setName);
binder.setBean(user);


Now it’s automatic:

When the user types in the nameField, the user.name property is updated.

When you call user.setName("Ali"), the field also updates automatically.

That’s data binding — linking UI ↔ model.

🎯 Why We Use Data Binding
| Reason                 | Explanation                                                             |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Less boilerplate**   | No need to manually transfer data between UI and backend.               |
| **Automatic sync**     | Changes in UI reflect in model and vice versa.                          |
| **Validation support** | Frameworks (like Vaadin) can validate fields easily through the binder. |
| **Cleaner code**       | Keeps code more readable and maintainable.                              |
| **Reactive UI**        | Real-time UI updates when model changes.                                |

🧩 Types of Data Binding
| Type                | Description                       | Example                                |
| ------------------- | --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| **One-way binding** | Data flows from model → UI        | Show a label of user name              |
| **Two-way binding** | Data flows both ways (UI ↔ model) | Form input updates model automatically |
| **Event binding**   | UI triggers an action in code     | Button click calls backend function    |

⚙️ Examples in Different Frameworks
| Framework      | Example                                                           |
| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Vaadin**     | `binder.bind(textField, User::getName, User::setName)`            |
| **Angular**    | `<input [(ngModel)]="user.name">`                                 |
| **React (JS)** | `<input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />`  |
| **JavaFX**     | `textField.textProperty().bindBidirectional(user.nameProperty())` |

🧾 Summary

Data Binding = automatic link between UI and data model
→ Keeps them synchronized, reduces code, and makes UIs dynamic and reactive.